From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 20:47:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 155/190] A successful visit leaves the drive paused, not
 playing

"The disk is being accessed the whole time after the first click."
Nothing is streaming -- the desktop reads nothing after the window is
drawn. ROMREADN commands play, nothing in the visit ever commanded
anything else, and the 60-second standby talks through our own VBL
commander, which is off while the timeshare is armed. So the drive
kept reading into the void for the rest of the session: access light
on, head drifting downstream, and every next visit paying a seek back
to the data.

CDBPAUSE at the end of every successful visit, followed by a few of
the firmware's own exchanges so the command actually reaches the drive
-- a posted command with no exchange behind it is the posted-to-nobody
mistake this file already made once. Sega's flow pauses between reads,
and a read issued from the pause hover starts instantly, so this
should also shave the seek-back off every subsequent folder and file
access. Failure paths skip it: their firmware may be mid-call.

Headless: nine rounds read the file, and the drive state in the
snapshots now shows 4 -- paused -- where it used to show 1, playing.
---
 bios/scdapi.h  |  2 +-
 bios/segacd2.S | 30 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 2 files changed, 30 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/bios/scdapi.h b/bios/scdapi.h
index 9564150..52d03da 100644
--- a/bios/scdapi.h
+++ b/bios/scdapi.h
@@ -28,7 +28,7 @@
 #define SCDAPI_H
 
 #define SCD_COOKIE   0x53674344L        /* 'SgCD' */
-#define SCD_VERSION  23
+#define SCD_VERSION  24
 
 /* Everything the disc diagnostic prints, in one go. Taken in a single
  * call rather than twenty accessors so that the numbers in one report
diff --git a/bios/segacd2.S b/bios/segacd2.S
index 9654a7e..68582a8 100644
--- a/bios/segacd2.S
+++ b/bios/segacd2.S
@@ -738,7 +738,7 @@ brd_read:
         addi.l  #0x0800,8(a5)
         subq.l  #1,4(a5)
         bne     brd_stat
-        bra     brd_done                // stage stays zero: all delivered
+        bra     brd_park                // delivered: settle the drive
 
         // Each stage names itself: 2 the firmware never reported ready,
         // 3 no sector was ever announced, 4 one was announced and never
@@ -776,6 +776,34 @@ brd_fail_read:
         // interrupt frame thrown away. Either way a5 and the scratch
         // stack are the only things it may assume, which is why the
         // stack it has to go back to lives at 44(a5) and not in a6.
+        // A successful visit leaves the drive paused, not playing.
+        //
+        // ROMREADN commands play and nothing here ever commanded
+        // anything else, so the drive kept reading into the void after
+        // every visit: the access light on for the rest of the session,
+        // the head drifting downstream, the next visit paying a seek
+        // back. The 60-second standby never helped because it talks
+        // through our own VBL commander, and the commander is off while
+        // the timeshare is armed.
+        //
+        // CDBPAUSE, then a few of the firmware's own exchanges so the
+        // command actually reaches the drive -- a posted command with
+        // no exchange behind it is the posted-to-nobody mistake again.
+        // Sega's flow pauses between reads, and a read issued from the
+        // pause hover starts instantly. Failure paths skip this: their
+        // firmware may be mid-call and their drive state is unknown.
+brd_park:
+        move.w  #0x0084,d0              // CDBPAUSE
+        move.w  d0,50(a5)
+        jsr     0x5F22
+        move.l  _segacd_bios_frames,d2
+        addq.l  #6,d2                   // ~0.1 s of exchanges
+        move.l  #0x60000,d3             // ...bounded by spin regardless
+1:      subq.l  #1,d3
+        beq     brd_done
+        cmp.l   _segacd_bios_frames,d2
+        bhi     1b
+
 brd_done:
         ori.w   #0x0700,sr              // shut the door before swapping back
 
